{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issues_index.json\\\")); print(type(d).__name__); print(str(d)[:12000])'\"","aggregated_output":"list\n[{'number': 1, 'title': 'Will there be a license?', 'created_at': '2020-01-23T19:34:37Z', 'closed_at': '2020-01-26T15:31:06Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"First off, _AWESOME_ project. I'll definitely be tinkering with this.\\r\\n\\r\\nQuestion: will you be applying a license to the code? I'm not here to push any license, but I do think at least _having_ a license is a good idea.\\r\\n\\r\\nGood job on the project 👏 👏 \"}, {'number': 2, 'title': 'Great lib!  Trouble with width', 'created_at': '2020-01-26T03:01:29Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-09T11:50:01Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"I'm really liking this library!  \\r\\n\\r\\nOne thing ... I seem to be having trouble with tables with widths greater than 80 chars.  Tried the various width options, but no luck.  Thoughts?\\r\\n\\r\\nThanks.\"}, {'number': 3, 'title': 'Markdown tables', 'created_at': '2020-01-26T16:28:59Z', 'closed_at': '2020-05-28T13:58:04Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"Hi @willmcgugan! Looks like an awesome library. I'm really interested in using this for terminal rendering of Markdown files, but I need to be able to render Markdown tables. Is that something you think would fit in this library?\"}, {'number': 4, 'title': 'Document whether Windows is supported', 'created_at': '2020-01-26T19:07:41Z', 'closed_at': '2020-01-27T16:23:38Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"I can't tell from the documentation, or the PyPI classifiers, whether Windows is supported. I'd like it if it were, but obviously that's up to you as a developer. But could you document more clearly whether you do, so that I don't have to download the library and try it out to check?\"}, {'number': 6, 'title': 'README.md - table.add_column error in readme', 'created_at': '2020-01-29T12:53:38Z', 'closed_at': '2020-01-29T13:36:41Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': 'In readme there is a row that is wrong in the add_column.\\r\\n\\r\\nFrom:\\r\\ntable.add_columngit (\"Production Budget\", justify=\"right\")\\r\\n\\r\\nTo:\\r\\ntable.add_column (\"Production Budget\", justify=\"right\")\\r\\n'}, {'number': 7, 'title': 'Console width off by one on some terminals / ssh', 'created_at': '2020-02-04T10:07:43Z', 'closed_at': '2020-02-04T18:32:13Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': 'On some consoles the output width seems to be calculated one character too wide. See the following example:\\r\\n```\\r\\n>>> from rich.console import Console\\r\\n>>> from rich.table import Table\\r\\n>>> c = Console()\\r\\n>>> t = Table(\"Yo!\", \"Tables!\")\\r\\n>>> t.add_row(\"a\", \"row\")\\r\\n>>> t.add_row(\"another\", \"row\")\\r\\n>>> c.print(t)\\r\\n┏━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━┓\\r\\n\\r\\n┃ Yo!     ┃ Tables! ┃\\r\\n\\r\\n┡━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━┩\\r\\n\\r\\n│ a       │ row     │\\r\\n\\r\\n│ another │ row     │\\r\\n\\r\\n└─────────┴─────────┘\\r\\n\\r\\n>>> c = Console(width=c.width-1)\\r\\n>>> c.print(t)\\r\\n┏━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━┓\\r\\n┃ Yo!     ┃ Tables! ┃\\r\\n┡━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━┩\\r\\n│ a       │ row     │\\r\\n│ another │ row     │\\r\\n└─────────┴─────────┘\\r\\n```\\r\\nI played around a bit and tested some combinations, some seem fine, others not:\\r\\n\\r\\n- ssh terminal in vscode: OK\\r\\n- ssh terminal in ConEmu: one too wide\\r\\n- ssh terminal in Windows command interpreter (Win+R, cmd): one too wide\\r\\n\\r\\nSource System is Win10, the system the console runs on was either Ubuntu 18 or Oracle Linux\\r\\n\\r\\nA possible workaround is as shown above: get a Console, and then get another one which is one narrower than the first one.\\r\\n\\r\\nLooking ath the code, i understand that you are retrieving the terminal size via `shutil.get_terminal_size()`, so maybe that issue is beyond the reach of the lib.'}, {'number': 8, 'title': 'Traceback output issue when code uses tab indentation', 'created_at': '2020-02-24T14:15:12Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-03T17:55:05Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"![Cmder_2020-02-24_09-12-37](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/118418/75159368-fec45280-56e5-11ea-9883-4476dc1bdb0f.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThat's what happens to the right border for the code lines when the code uses tabs for indentation.\"}, {'number': 9, 'title': 'How to construct a console with styles?', 'created_at': '2020-02-24T19:07:00Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-09T11:48:59Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': 'Hi! 👋 \\r\\n\\r\\nI\\'ve defined a console and theme like so. I think there\\'s some out of date docs around constructing consoles, so I\\'ve read the code and this is what I got to:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nfrom rich.theme import Theme, Style as St\\r\\n\\r\\ntheme = Theme({\\r\\n    \"pass-tag\": St.parse(\"white on green\"),\\r\\n    \"fail-tag\": St.parse(\"white on red\"),\\r\\n    \"skip-tag\": St.parse(\"white on blue\"),\\r\\n    \"xfail-tag\": St.parse(\"white on magenta\"),\\r\\n    \"xpass-tag\": St.parse(\"white on yellow\"),\\r\\n    \"dryrun-tag\": St.parse(\"white on green\"),\\r\\n    \"test-location\": St.parse(\"dim white\"),\\r\\n})\\r\\n\\r\\nconsole = Console(theme=theme)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIs this the intended usage?\\r\\n\\r\\nP.S. Happy to make a PR updating the docs if so! :)'}, {'number': 10, 'title': 'Underscores are being stripped out incorrectly in table cells', 'created_at': '2020-02-24T19:34:45Z', 'closed_at': '2020-02-24T20:52:10Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': 'I\\'ve got a table which I created like so:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n    table = Table(show_header=False, padding=0, show_edge=False, box=0)\\r\\n    table.add_column(\"outcome\", style=theme, width=6, justify=\"center\")\\r\\n    table.add_column(\"location\", style=\"test-location\")\\r\\n    table.add_column(\"description\", style=\"none\")\\r\\n\\r\\n    table.add_row(outcome, location, f\"{description} {reason}\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIf the `description` contains two underscores, such as in many Python function names, the underscores are removed.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor example, in the following screenshot, the top line is the description string. The bottom line is the description as displayed in the table.\\r\\n\\r\\n<img width=\"729\" alt=\"Screenshot 2020-02-24 at 19 33 08\" src=\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5740731/75184571-82109300-573c-11ea-92be-0dd4fcbf7ca3.png\">\\r\\n\\r\\nThis *doesn\\'t* happen if the description contains a single `_`. I was guessing it was trying to render it as Markdown or something, but I get the same issue when setting `markup=False` when initialising the `Console`. 😄 \\r\\n '}, {'number': 11, 'title': 'Setting up coverage reporting via CI / linting?', 'created_at': '2020-02-24T20:34:35Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-03T16:10:24Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"1. Are you interested on having coverage reports via CI?\\r\\n\\r\\nI suggest using codecov for that (with a config that disables its comments, so that only PR/commit statuses are reported).\\r\\n\\r\\nI also suggest having a tox configuration for this then, so that `tox -e py38-coverage` could be used on CI, but also locally in the same way.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Are you interested in having some linting via CI / tox also?\\r\\nFor that using flake8 might be good enough, but would require some fixes / running black/autopep8 once.\\r\\n\\r\\nI'd be happy to contribute whatever you prefer.\"}, {'number': 12, 'title': 'Issue with `end` parameter', 'created_at': '2020-02-25T18:33:51Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-09T11:50:17Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': 'I\\'m looking for a way to write something to my terminal without automatically taking a newline. With the `print` builtin, I can pass an empty string as the `end` paramter. The `end` parameter in `Console.print` doesn\\'t seem to work in the same way however.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Example:**\\r\\n```python\\r\\nprint(\".\", end=\"\")\\r\\nprint(\".\", end=\"\")\\r\\nprint(\".\", end=\"\")\\r\\n\\r\\nconsole.print(\".\", end=\"\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\".\", end=\"\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\".\", end=\"\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n....\\r\\n.\\r\\n.\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Expected output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n......\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also seems to add a space before the `end` character:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Example:**\\r\\n```python\\r\\nprint(\".\", end=\"x\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\".\", end=\"x\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.x. x\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Expected output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.x.x\\r\\n```'}, {'number': 13, 'title': '`sep` parameter is inconsistent with builtin `print`', 'created_at': '2020-02-25T18:41:20Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-09T11:50:36Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': '`sep` behaves differently from builtin print. I don\\'t know if this is intentional or not, but the behaviour is slightly different from how the docs describe it. (I didn\\'t expect the `sep` to be added to the *end* of the string, only *between* the printed items).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Example:**\\r\\n```python\\r\\nprint(\".\", \".\", sep=\"a\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\".\", \".\", sep=\"a\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.a.\\r\\n.a.a\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Expected output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.a.\\r\\n.a.\\r\\n```'}, {'number': 14, 'title': 'Progress bar functionality', 'created_at': '2020-03-05T23:46:06Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-12T21:23:32Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"Any plans/thoughts on including progress bar functionality?\\r\\n\\r\\nI know they're another big pill to swallow, but they certainly could fit the premise of rich. Integrating outside progress bar libraries isn't great as it is right now.\"}, {'number': 15, 'title': 'Add rich.__version__', 'created_at': '2020-03-08T02:38:48Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-08T12:35:35Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"`rich` should have `__version__` property.\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\nAttributeError: module 'rich' has no attribute '__version__'\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is a common python convention to tell package version easily.\"}, {'number': 16, 'title': 'rich.traceback -- do not wrap long lines', 'created_at': '2020-03-08T02:45:44Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-08T13:09:52Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': '`rich.traceback` makes stacktrace nicer but it wraps everything in 80 characters.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor example, see a screenshot in your blog post -- https://www.willmcgugan.com/blog/tech/post/better-python-tracebacks-with-rich/.\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\\r\\n│ File                                                                                             │\\r\\n│\"/home/USERNAME/.miniconda3/envs/longer-paths-for-reasons/lib/python3.7/site-packages/numpy/core/f│\\r\\n│line 1477, in squeeze                                                                             │\\r\\n    (... omitted)\\r\\n│\\r\\n╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nAs it truncates the full stacktrace line at certain characters, it becomes inconvenient for users to see which file to look at. I suggest we should not wrap them inside a box.'}, {'number': 17, 'title': 'Long string wrapping in table columns', 'created_at': '2020-03-08T19:29:14Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-09T18:14:24Z', 'state_reason': 'completed', 'body': \"So, long strings (exceeding the width of the console) aren't wrapped and result in output like:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n cf69f8a0618f3bb8ba0ed2cc31d730827b09c3ca\\r\\n\\r\\n README.md\\r\\n\\r\\n 2.91 KiB\\r\\n\\r\\n 16 KiB\\r\\n\\r\\n 1\\r\\n\\r\\n Yes\\r\\n 2020-03-08 15:27:37 -04:00\\r\\n\\r\\n thorod 2.0.0\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n magnet:?dn=README.md&xt=urn:btih:cf69f8a0618f3bb8ba0ed2cc31d730827b09c3ca&xl=2984&tr=udp://tracker.opentrackr.org:1337/announce&tr=udp://9.rarbg.to:2710/an\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nwhere the string is cut and the left column disappears instead of something like:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n Info Hash:      212769dc7fdac4030d93b517720914fd575b3ce1\\r\\n Torrent Name:   README.md\\r\\n Data Size:      2.91 KiB\\r\\n Piece Size:     16 KiB\\r\\n Piece Count:    1\\r\\n Private:        Yes\\r\\n Creation Date:  2020-03-08 15:27:07 -04:00\\r\\n Created By:     thorod 2.0.0\\r\\n Comment:\\r\\n Source:\\r\\n Magnet:         magnet:?dn=README.md&xt=urn:btih:212769dc7fdac4030d93b517720914fd575b3ce1&xl=2984&tr=udp://explodie.org:6969/announce\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nShould rich be wrapping these itself?\"}, {'number': 18, 'title': 'Markdown markup syntax interferes with some filenames', 'created_at': '2020-03-09T15:04:40Z', 'closed_at': '2020-03-11T13:19:56Z', 'state_reason': 'compl\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
